== 14. God Is Omnipresent

Fourteenthly, consider that God is omnipresent. We cannot get into any blind hole, dark corner, or secret place where the Lord does not have an eye; the Lord will keep us company there. “And your Father, who sees in secret, will reward you openly” (Matt 6:6, 18). A saint cannot creep into the darkest, dirtiest hole in the world without God having a favorable eye there. God never lacks an eye to see our secret tears, or an ear to hear our secret cries and groans, or a heart to grant our secret requests; and therefore we ought to pour out our souls to Him in secret. “O Lord, my every desire is before You; my groaning is not hidden from You” (Ps 38:9). However confused our private desires are, however broken our private requests are, and however much our private groanings are hidden from men, yet God eyes them all, God records them all, and God puts them all on file in heaven, and will one day crown them with glorious answers and returns. We cannot sigh out a prayer in secret without Him seeing us; we cannot lift up our eyes to Him at midnight without Him observing us. The eye that God has on His people when they are in secret is such a special, tender eye of love that it opens His ear, His heart, and His hand for their good. “For the eyes of the Lord are on the righteous, and His ears are open to their prayers”—or as the Greek has it, “His ears are to their prayers” (1 Pet 3:12). If their prayers are so faint that they cannot reach up as high as heaven, then God will bow the heavens and come down to their prayers.

God’s eye is on every secret sigh, and every secret groan, and every secret tear, and every secret desire, and every secret pant of love, and every secret breathing of soul, and every secret melting and working of heart. All this should encourage us to be much in secret duties, in closet services. As a Christian is never out of the reach of God’s hand, so he is never out of the view of God’s eye. If a Christian cannot hide himself from the sun, which is God’s minister of light, how impossible it will be for him to hide himself from Him Whose eyes are ten thousand times brighter than the sun! In every private duty, a Christian is always under the eye of God’s omniscience. When we are in the darkest hole, God has windows into our breasts and observes all the secret actions of our inner man (1 Tim 2:8). The eye of God is not confined to this place or that, to this company or that. God has an eye on His people as much when they are alone as when they are among a multitude, as much when they are in a corner as when they are in a crowd. Diana’s temple was burned down when she was busy at Alexander’s birth and could not be in two places at once. But God is present both in paradise and in the wilderness, both in the family and in the closet, both in public and in private at the same time. God is an omnipresent God; He is everywhere. “There is nowhere where God is not.” As He is enclosed in no place, so He is shut out of no place. “Can a man hide in secret places where I cannot see him?” declares the #smallcaps[Lord] (Jer 23:24). “The eyes of the #smallcaps[Lord] are in every place, observing the evil and the good,” or, “contemplating the evil and the good,” as the Hebrew may be read (Prov 15:3). Now, to contemplate is more than simply to observe, for contemplation adds to a simple perception a deeper degree of knowledge, entering into the very inside of a matter. And so indeed God discerns the very inward intentions of the heart and the most secret stirrings of the spirit. God is an infinite and immense being Whose center is everywhere and Whose circumference is nowhere. Now, if our God is omnipresent, then wherever we are, our God is present with us. If we are in prison alone with Joseph, our God is present with us there; or if we are in exile alone with David, our God is present with us there; or if we are alone in our closets, our God is present with us there. God sees us in secret, and therefore let us seek His face in secret. Heaven is God’s palace, but it is not His prison.
